  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 215 AM EST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin Mon evening and there is still a good \ndeal of uncertainty with the whats going to occur next week. Both \nthe GFS and the ECMWF show a few waves traversing across the area in \nthe forecast period but the uncertain is where, when, and how much. \n\nThings kick off Mon evening with a decaying frontal boundary moving \nslowly SE across the area and a wave of moisture pulling NE out of \nthe gulf across central and south GA through Tues. the models are in \nagreement that there will be something moving through Mon night/Tue \nbut the ECMWF is much drier than the GFS. For now, leaning towards \nthe dry/low rain chance solutions given the weak forcing and dry air \nin place, but confidence is still low. \n\nConfidence in the forecast stays low from Tuesday night on as the \nGFS shows a strong wave sliding out of the Southern Plains across \nthe Gulf Coast Wednesday as a second wave moves NE out of the \ncentral Gulf across central/southern FL. The ECMWF only shows the \nwave moving NE out of the Central Gulf but it shows it moving more \nacross southern AL into GA and across FL. The track, speed, and \nstrength of this system, as well as how it interacts with a wedge in \nplace over Georgia is sill uncertain, as this will determine what we \nget across North and Central GA Wed through Fri. The only thing we \ncan say with any confidence is that these solutions will create a \nperiod of moist S-SW flow over Georgia with widespread rain chances. \nThe details of this, including how long the rain chances last, where \nthey are highest, and how intense the rainfall is, should become \nclearer in the next few day. The range of potential outcomes is \nlarge, with some solutions having the initial system move through \nquickly with little rainfall (0.1-0.25\" or less), while others on \nthe high end have a stronger system developing with heavy rain and \ngusty winds for portions of our area. Despite all these \nuncertainties, the overall pattern will support a cooling trend, \nwith highs in the 50s to 60s and lows returning to the 30s by the \nend of the week. Stay tuned for updates, especially if you have \noutdoor plans for the Wed-Fri time period.",
